// Runtime/DebugStreamRuntime.maxon — the DebugStream PRODUCER (Workstream O3).
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE SPLIT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN, AND IT IS NOT SHV2'S TO CHOOSE.** `maxon monitor` — the C#
// bootstrap's `DebugStreamMonitor` / `DebugStreamDecoder` — is the CONSUMER: it creates the shared
// segment, spawns the target with `MAXON_DEBUGSTREAM` naming it, and decodes and prints every event.
// This file is the PRODUCER, and there is no decoder anywhere in shv2. Two processes, one wire format,
// and the format is FROZEN: every constant below is read off the reference
// (`maxon-sharp/Compiler/MLIR/Runtime/RuntimeEmitter.cs`) rather than chosen here, because a producer
// that disagrees with its consumer about a shift does not fail to build — it makes the trace LIE.
//
// ⭐ **WHAT IT IS FOR.** `specs-shv2/mm-trace.md` asserts a program's ALLOCATION BEHAVIOUR: not what it
// computed, but how many records it allocated and what its refcount column did. No exit code can say
// that. A `StringBuilder` that pays one `ByteArray` per `append` computes exactly the right answer, and
// the only instrument that can see the regression is a trace of the memory manager itself.
//
// ⭐ **THE GATING RULE, FROM COMMIT 1 (PLAN.md's O3 box).** Two levels, and both are load-bearing:
//   • **COMPILE TIME — `--debugstream` off implies ZERO INSTRUCTIONS.** Not a branch that is never
//     taken: no call, no store, no header word. A normal build's emitted code is byte-identical to what
//     it was before this file existed, which is what keeps the suite's committed `.test` fragment
//     goldens from drifting. Every emitter below is a no-op when `debugStream` is false, exactly as
//     `GtRuntime.emitGtTraceLine` is when the build is untraced, so each call site reads as an
//     unconditional statement and the gating lives in one place.
//   • **RUN TIME — `__ds_base == 0` implies an INLINE BAIL BEFORE ANY CALL.** A `--debugstream` binary
//     run with no monitor attached pays one load and one branch per event site, never a call. The
//     reference pays two CALLs before its runtime-off check; that wart is not reproduced.
//
// ⭐ **THE RING PROTOCOL, IN THREE MOMENTS — because two of them are one moment in the obvious design,
// and that design fabricates events.**
//   1. RESERVE (`__ds_reserve`, under the ring lock). Claim the bytes, write the entry HEADER with the
//      commit bit CLEAR, advance `write_cursor`, drop the lock. The entry is now VISIBLE to the
//      monitor. Its payload is still whatever the ring last held there.
//   2. PAYLOAD (the caller, outside the lock).
//   3. COMMIT (`__ds_commit`). Set the commit bit. The entry is now READABLE, and only now.
// The monitor decodes nothing — and advances past nothing — that does not carry the commit bit. Before
// that bit existed the reference measured 0 events decoded, 283221 dropped and 5290 abandoned, every
// number of it a fiction. `emitDsEntry` below is the ONLY caller of `__ds_reserve` and it always pairs
// the reserve with a commit, so the pairing is the only shape a call site can have rather than a
// convention each one is trusted to follow.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE LOCK IS A CRITICAL_SECTION, NOT THE REFERENCE'S TICKET SPINLOCK, AND THAT IS A DIVERGENCE
// WITH A REASON.** A ticket lock is `atomic_xadd` twice, and the Std tier has NO ATOMIC OP AT ALL — not
// one, in the whole dialect. shv2 already owns a real OS mutex (`osLockInit`/`osLockEnter`/`osLockLeave`,
// the scheduler's `__sched_lock`), which serializes the critical section exactly as the ticket lock
// does and needs no vocabulary this tier does not have. Reproducing the reference's spin would mean
// adding atomics to the IR for one debug-only producer, which is the "a COPY needs a reason exactly as
// much as a DIVERGENCE does" rule pointing the other way.
//
// ⚠ **THE FLAG ALONE DOES NOT DECIDE WHETHER A BUILD CARRIES THIS: `buildCarriesDebugStream` DOES**, and
// it is `--debugstream` AND `usesHeap`. Four parts must agree about that answer and they are in three
// files, so it is asked rather than re-spelled — see that function for the reason `usesHeap` is half of
// it and for what a drift between the four would cost.

// ============================================================================
// The wire contract. FROZEN — see the header. Every constant is the reference's.
// ============================================================================

// `"MXDBSTR\0"` — the segment's first word, written by the monitor when it creates the segment and
// checked here before this producer writes a byte. It is what tells a DebugStream ring from somebody
// else's shared memory that happens to be named the same thing.
let DsMagic = 0x4D58444253545200

// ⭐⭐ **THE WIRE SCHEMA VERSION — the one number the two ends must agree on, and the only thing between
// a mismatch and a trace that LIES.** v2 introduced the entry COMMIT BIT, which was not an additive
// change: under v1 the flags byte was always zero and "below `write_cursor`" MEANT readable, so the two
// schemas disagree about what an entry with flags == 0 IS. Neither end can tell the other's schema by
// looking at the ring, so each ANNOUNCES its version and refuses to speak across a mismatch. Bump this
// only in lockstep with the reference's `DsVersion`.
let DsSchemaVersion = 2

// The segment's three regions. The monitor CREATES the section exactly `DsSharedMemoryBytes` long and
// this producer MAPS it exactly that long, in two different languages — and a `MapViewOfFile` longer
// than the section returns NULL, so a disagreement here does not fail to build: the trace simply never
// appears. ONE definition, derived from the three parts rather than written out.
//
// ⚠ `DsTagReserveBytes` is a region NOTHING WRITES: it held v1's in-band tag table, and the interned
// names now live in the executable's own `.symtab` (see the MXDS_TAGS blob below). It stays part of the
// layout because both processes must agree on the mapping LENGTH, and shrinking it buys nothing.
let DsHeaderBytes = 128
let DsRingBytes = 2097152
let DsTagReserveBytes = 65536
let DsSharedMemoryBytes = DsHeaderBytes + DsRingBytes + DsTagReserveBytes

// Segment-header field offsets, from the base of the mapping.
let DsOffMagic = 0 as ByteOffset

// The MONITOR's schema version, written when it creates the segment.
let DsOffVersion = 8 as ByteOffset
let DsOffBufferSize = 16 as ByteOffset
let DsOffWriteCursor = 24 as ByteOffset
let DsOffReadCursor = 32 as ByteOffset
let DsOffFlags = 40 as ByteOffset
let DsOffStartTimestamp = 56 as ByteOffset
let DsOffTotalEvents = 64 as ByteOffset
let DsOffDroppedEvents = 72 as ByteOffset
let DsOffPeakUsed = 96 as ByteOffset

// ⭐ The PRODUCER's schema version. `DsOffVersion` above is the MONITOR's — the two travel in opposite
// directions, because it takes both for each end to check the other. A producer too old to know this slot
// exists leaves it at zero, and "wrote entries but never announced a version" is precisely the signature
// of one.
let DsOffProducerVersion = 104 as ByteOffset

// Header flags bit 0 — a producer is attached and writing.
let DsFlagProducerAlive = 1

// ============================================================================
// Entry header: ONE packed u64.
//   [0:7]   event_type
//   [8:15]  flags        — bit 0 is THE COMMIT BIT
//   [16:31] entry_size   (total bytes, header included, 8-byte aligned)
//   [32:63] timestamp_delta (ms since the header's start_timestamp)
// ============================================================================
let DsEntryFlagsShift = 8
let DsEntrySizeShift = 16
let DsEntryTimestampShift = 32

// ⭐⭐ **THE COMMIT BIT, ALREADY POSITIONED INSIDE THE PACKED HEADER WORD.** An entry below
// `write_cursor` is PRESENT; only an entry carrying this bit is READABLE. See the ring-protocol note in
// this file's header for what happens without it.
let DsEntryCommittedBit = 256

// A PADDING entry fills the ring's tail when the next real entry would straddle the wrap. It is written
// ENTIRELY inside `__ds_reserve`, has no payload and no second writer — so it is BORN COMMITTED: nothing
// would ever come back to set its bit, and a monitor that refuses to advance past an uncommitted entry
// would stall on it forever. These are the low 16 bits of its header, event_type | flags.
//
// Its event_type is the reference's, exactly like the mm codes below — named rather than left a literal
// beside four named siblings, because it is the same kind of wire fact they are.
let DsEvPadding = 255
let DsPaddingHeaderTypeAndFlags = DsEvPadding + DsEntryCommittedBit

// ============================================================================
// The mm event family. Codes 0x01-0x04; every one is 24 bytes with the same shape.
// ============================================================================
export let DsEvMmAlloc = 1
export let DsEvMmFree = 2
export let DsEvMmIncref = 3
export let DsEvMmDecref = 4

// Offsets from the START of the entry, so the header's 8 bytes are included and an emitter writes
// straight through the pointer `__ds_reserve` handed back.
let DsMmOffAllocId = 8 as ByteOffset

// `tag_index(2) | scope_len(2) | value(4)` — one word. `value` is the allocation SIZE for `mm_alloc` and
// the new OWNER COUNT for the refcount family; `scope_len` is 0 (this producer emits no scope names).
let DsMmOffPacked = 16 as ByteOffset
let DsMmValueShift = 32

// Header + alloc_id(8) + packed word(8). Already 8-byte aligned, so nothing rounds it.
let DsMmEntryBytes = 24

// ⭐ **THE `packed_id` THE MM RUNTIME CARRIES IN EVERY BOX HEADER: `alloc_id << 16 | tag_index`.**
// `mm_incref`/`mm_decref`/`mm_free` know only a POINTER — there is no tag and no id to be had at those
// call sites — so both facts are stamped into the box by `__mm_alloc` and read back out here. The mask is
// what bounds a tag index to the 16 bits the wire gives it.
export let DsMmPackedIdShift = 16
let DsMmTagIndexMask = 65535

// ============================================================================
// The LOG event family. Codes 0x60-0x63 — the only events a PROGRAM authors.
// ============================================================================
//
// ⭐⭐ **EVERY OTHER FAMILY IS THE RUNTIME TALKING ABOUT ITSELF; THESE FOUR ARE THE PROGRAM'S OWN
// SENTENCES**, written through the `__DebugStream` builtin (`Parser.parseCallNamed`'s DebugStream chain).
// They exist because shv2 IS a Maxon program: a text log to stderr stops being readable the moment N
// workers interleave into one stream, and cannot say which worker, which compile or which phase a line
// came from. Every one of them therefore carries `gt`, `p_id` and `unit_id`, which is what lets a reader
// demux them back apart.
//
// The payload after the 8-byte entry header, FROZEN like every other constant in this file — the table is
// the reference's (`RuntimeEmitter.cs`'s DebugStream band), because a producer that disagrees with its
// consumer about a shift does not fail to build, it makes the trace LIE:
//   0x60/0x61: gt(8) · p_id(8) · phase_id(2) rsvd(2) unit_id(4)                     — 32 bytes
//   0x62:      gt(8) · p_id(8) · cat(1) lvl(1) event_id(2) unit_id(4) · a0(8) a1(8) — 48 bytes
//   0x63:      gt(8) · p_id(8) · cat(1) lvl(1) len(2) unit_id(4) · UTF-8 tail       — variable
//
// ⭐ **THE TWO PHASE CODES ARE EXPORTED AND THE OTHER TWO ARE NOT, AND THAT IS THE SPLIT IN THE SURFACE.**
// `phaseBegin` and `phaseEnd` are ONE runtime entry taking the code as an argument (they differ in nothing
// else), so the LOWERING is what supplies it; `event` and `text` each have an entry of their own and their
// code never leaves this file.
export let DsEvLogPhaseBegin = 96
export let DsEvLogPhaseEnd = 97
let DsEvLogEvent = 98
let DsEvLogText = 99

// Offsets from the START of the entry, so the 8-byte header is included — the mm family's convention above.
let DsLogOffGt = 8 as ByteOffset
let DsLogOffPid = 16 as ByteOffset

// The packed field word. Its layout differs per event (see the table above), but every Log event has
// exactly one and it always sits here.
let DsLogOffFields = 24 as ByteOffset
let DsLogOffArg0 = 32 as ByteOffset
let DsLogOffArg1 = 40 as ByteOffset

// LOG_TEXT only: the first byte of the UTF-8 tail. It shares LOG_EVENT's `arg0` offset because the two
// entries diverge exactly there — one carries two numbers, the other a string.
let DsLogOffText = 32 as ByteOffset

let DsLogPhaseEntryBytes = 32
let DsLogEventEntryBytes = 48

// The FIXED part of a LOG_TEXT entry; the 8-byte-aligned UTF-8 tail follows it.
let DsLogTextFixedBytes = 32

// The packing of `DsLogOffFields`. LOG_EVENT and LOG_TEXT pack it IDENTICALLY — cat(1) lvl(1) u16(2)
// unit_id(4) — and only the meaning of the 16-bit slot differs (an interned `event_id` for one, a byte
// LENGTH for the other), which is why there is ONE shift and ONE mask for that slot rather than a pair
// each, and why `emitPackedLogFields` serves both.
//
// ⚠⚠ **LOG_PHASE PACKS A DIFFERENT WORD, AND THE REFERENCE'S OWN PROSE SAYS OTHERWISE.** Its comment reads
// *"LOG_PHASE_BEGIN/END use only the u16 slot (as `phase_id`) and the unit"*, which invites exactly the
// copy that would be wrong: its CODE masks `name_id` and ors it in UNSHIFTED, and its DECODER reads
// `fields & DsLogU16FieldMask`. So `phase_id` occupies bits [0:15] — the byte slots `cat` and `lvl` take in
// the other two — and a phase entry that reused `emitPackedLogFields` would put the name 16 bits too high.
// MEASURED by sabotage: shifted, every phase in the trace decodes as `name=0`, an event the instrument
// cannot name. Two layouts, two packings.
//
// ⚠ `unit_id` occupies the high 32 bits, so the shift itself discards anything that would not fit and it
// needs no mask. Saying so is what keeps a fourth mask from being added "for symmetry".
let DsLogCatMask = 255
let DsLogLvlShift = 8
let DsLogLvlMask = 255
let DsLogU16FieldShift = 16
let DsLogU16FieldMask = 65535
let DsLogUnitIdShift = 32

// The ring keeps every entry 8-byte aligned, so a LOG_TEXT's tail is rounded UP to this. The SHIFT is what
// the round is emitted as (`>> 3 << 3`) and the bias is what makes it round up rather than down.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE REASON THAT USED TO BE GIVEN FOR THE SHIFT PAIR — *"the mask form would need a bitwise
// complement this tier has no op for"* — IS FALSE, and was found by W215's review.** The mask is a
// COMPILE-TIME constant, so nothing has to be complemented at run time: `MmRuntime.emitAlignUp` emits
// exactly `(value + width - 1) and -width` over two `emitConst`s, and this very file already reaches for
// `StdBinOpcode.bitAnd` twice (`emitRingPos`, `emitMaskedField`). What the tier genuinely lacks is a
// complement of a RUNTIME value, which is why `SlabArena.emitBitRunUpdate` spells `not bit` as `bit xor -1`
// — a different situation that this comment had borrowed the excuse from.
//
// ⇒ **KNOWN DEBT, FILED RATHER THAN RIDDEN ALONG: this is a THIRD hand-spelling of the align-up idiom
// `emitAlignUp` was made the single owner of**, and one op more than it (add + shr + shl against add + and).
// Collapsing it moves EMITTED code in a runtime W215 does not own, so it wants its own gate over the
// debug-stream specs rather than a reviewer's commit.
let DsEntryAlign = 8
let DsEntryAlignShift = 3
let DsEntryAlignBias = DsEntryAlign - 1

// The entry header's `entry_size` is a u16, so no single entry can exceed what that field holds.
let DsEntrySizeMax = 65535

// The largest 8-ALIGNED entry that field can express. ⚠ Subtracting the bias is the round-down here and
// not a coincidence of 65535: a mask maximum is `2^k - 1`, which for every `k` above 2 is 7 modulo 8, so
// the largest multiple of 8 below it is always exactly `max - 7`.
let DsMaxAlignedEntryBytes = DsEntrySizeMax - DsEntryAlignBias

// ⭐ **THE LONGEST UTF-8 TAIL A LOG_TEXT CAN CARRY: TRUNCATE, NEVER TEAR.** What is left of a maximal
// ALIGNED entry after the fixed part — and the fixed part is itself a multiple of 8, so what is left stays
// aligned. A longer message is CLAMPED to this rather than split across two entries, which would need a
// continuation code the frozen schema does not have and a reader that reassembles it. Derived rather than
// written out, so it cannot drift from the constants it is a consequence of.
let DsLogTextMaxBytes = DsMaxAlignedEntryBytes - DsLogTextFixedBytes

// ============================================================================
// The MXDS_TAGS name blob — how a 16-bit tag index becomes a TYPE NAME.
// ============================================================================
//
// ⭐ **IT TRAVELS IN THE EXECUTABLE, NOT IN THE RING.** The monitor scans the PE's `.symtab` section for
// this magic and decodes the packed table behind it, so no name is ever built at run time and an event
// stays 24 fixed bytes. shv2's PE writer already emits a `.symtab` whenever `CodeResult.symdata` is
// non-empty; filling that array is the whole of the producer's side.
//
// Format: `[magic(10)][count:u16][len0:u16][name0]…[lenN:u16][nameN]`, little-endian.
// ⚠ **INDEX 0 MEANS "NO NAME"** — the monitor's own rule (`ResolveInternedName`), which is why
// `MmTag.Untagged` occupies it and why its blob entry is written EMPTY rather than with its case name.
let DsTagBlobMagic = b"MXDS_TAGS\0"

// ⭐⭐ **THE SECOND BLOB, IN THE SAME FORMAT AND THE SAME SECTION — the names `__DebugStream.nameId`
// INTERNED AT COMPILE TIME** (phase names, event names). A separate TABLE from `MXDS_TAGS` because the two
// answer different wire fields: an mm event carries a tag index into the tags table, and a Log event
// carries a name index into this one. Sharing one table would resolve both fields against one numbering and
// print an allocation's type where a phase name belongs.
//
// ⚠ **TWO BLOBS, ONE WRITER.** They differ in exactly one thing — these ten magic bytes — so
// `debugStreamNameBlob` takes the magic and there is no second byte-splitting loop to drift from this one.
// The MONITOR finds each by SCANNING the section for its magic (`DebugStreamDecode.ScanForNameBlob`), so
// the two may sit end to end in one `symdataImage` here where the bootstrap defines two labelled payloads.
let DsStrBlobMagic = b"MXDS_STRS\0"

// The width of EVERY count and length field in the blob — `writeWord`'s width, named here because it is
// what the MONITOR steps by when it parses the table back out of the executable. It is stated for the
// refusal message and for the reader; the bytes themselves come from `writeWord`, so there is no second
// splitting loop to drift from it.
let DsNameBlobFieldBytes = 2
let DsNameBlobFieldMax = 65535

// ============================================================================
// The FIXED tag roster — every allocation the RUNTIME ITSELF makes.
// ============================================================================
//
// ⭐ **`.ordinal` IS THE WIRE INDEX AND `.name` IS THE DISPLAYED NAME, so a tag is ONE fact.** A separate
// display table beside the enum would be the second copy this project keeps finding drifted; here a case
// added in the wrong place moves an index the blob is generated from, so both ends move together.
//
// ⚠ **APPEND ONLY, AND `Untagged` STAYS AT ZERO.** The blob is generated from this order, so a reorder
// re-points every tag in the trace — and index 0 is the monitor's "no name", not a case like the others.
//
// ⚠ **THE NAMES DESCRIBE THE BOX, NOT THE SURFACE TYPE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.** A runtime chunk
// allocating an array's element buffer does not know the element type — nothing at that tier does — so
// `ElementBuffer` is the honest answer where `ByteArray` would be a guess. USER type names are not in
// this roster at all: they are interned dynamically off the allocation's own result type (see
// `userTagIndex` and `LowerMaxonToStd`'s `__mm_alloc` arm), which is where `StringBuilder`
// comes from.
export enum MmTag
	Untagged
	StringRecord
	ArrayRecord
	ElementBuffer
	CString
	ListRecord
	ListNode
	CursorRecord
	FileBox
	DirectoryBox
	// ⭐ **THE THREE BOXES THE LOWERING CANNOT NAME FROM A TYPE, so the PARSER names them** — through the
	// `Project.allocTagSites` SIDE TABLE, keyed by the `__mm_alloc` op, never through the value's type (see
	// that field for what happened when the label rode in the type slot). Each is a compiler-minted heap
	// record with no user type behind it: the byte run an interpolated part is rendered into, the
	// environment a closure's captures live in, and the one-slot cell a reassigned by-reference parameter is
	// promoted into. All three arrive at the lowering tagged `integer` with no name id, which is why an
	// INDEX-0 `Untagged` was what a trace used to show for them — an allocation the instrument could not
	// name.
	InterpolationScratch
	ClosureEnv
	ParameterCell
end 'MmTag'

// The wire index of a fixed roster tag — the ordinal, named once so no call site spells `.ordinal` at a
// place where the reader would have to know that the ordinal IS the index.
export function mmTagIndex(tag MmTag) returns ParsedInt
	return tag.ordinal as ParsedInt
end 'mmTagIndex'

// ============================================================================
// The tag table's FORMAT — the roster, the blob, and the one door a user name comes through.
// ============================================================================

export typealias DebugStreamNameIndexMap = Map with (String, MachineWord)

// ⭐⭐ **ONE TABLE KIND, TWO INSTANCES — `MXDS_TAGS` AND `MXDS_STRS`.** Both answer the same question for
// the same wire width (a name ⇒ a u16 the monitor resolves back), differ only in WHICH names they hold, and
// were two byte-identical interners on `GlobalDataTable` for exactly as long as there was one of them.
//
// ⚠ **DEDUP IS THE POINT, NOT AN OPTIMIZATION**: it is what keeps a blob proportional to the DISTINCT names
// a program mentions rather than to its mention SITES — the tag table would otherwise carry one entry per
// allocation site and the name table one per `nameId` call.
//
// ⚠ **THE 16-BIT BOUND IS CHECKED WHERE THE BLOB IS WRITTEN, NOT HERE.** A count that will not fit is
// exactly an index that will not fit, so a second test beside the minter would be the same fact twice
// (`appendNameBlobField`).
export type DebugStreamNameTable
	// The wire table: `names[i]` is the name index `i` resolves to. Read by `debugStreamNameBlob`.
	export var names as StringArray
	// Its inverse, so interning an already-seen name is O(1) rather than a scan of `names`.
	var byName as DebugStreamNameIndexMap

	// ⚠ **THE SEED IS INDEXED TOO, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES INDEX 0 UNREACHABLE BY INTERNING.** Both rosters
	// reserve index 0 for the monitor's "no name" (`ResolveInternedName` skips it), and mapping the seed
	// means a later `intern` of the same spelling ANSWERS 0 rather than minting a second entry for it. For
	// the tag table that is unreachable — no type is named `""`. For the interned-name table it is the
	// answer `__DebugStream.nameId("")` gets, and it is the right one: an empty name IS no name.
	// ⚠ The bootstrap mints 1 there instead (its map starts empty), and then renders it as `name=1` because
	// its own resolver rejects an empty entry — so the two agree that the name is unresolvable and disagree
	// only about the number. `specs-shv2/debugstream-name-id.md` pins shv2's.
	export static function create(seed StringArray) returns DebugStreamNameTable
		var byName = DebugStreamNameIndexMap.create()
		for name in seed 'seedIndex'
			byName.upsert(name, value: byName.count())
		end 'seedIndex'
		return Self{names: seed, byName: byName}
	end 'create'

	// The wire index for `name`, minting one on first sight.
	export function intern(name String) returns ParsedInt
		return try self.byName.get(name) otherwise 'fresh'
			let minted = self.names.count()
			self.byName.upsert(name, value: minted)
			self.names.push(name)
			return minted as ParsedInt
		end 'fresh'
	end 'intern'
end 'DebugStreamNameTable'

// ⭐ **BOTH TABLE INSTANCES LIVE ON `GlobalDataTable`, NOT HERE, and that is deliberate.** It is the tree's
// one shared, aliased, mutable carrier of "what goes into the emitted sections" — the same object `.rdata`
// payloads and the `.data` layout are registered into — so it is already reachable both from the LOWERING
// that interns (a user type's name, a `nameId` literal) and from the BACKEND that renders the blob. A second
// carrier threaded alongside it would be a second thing every one of those sites had to be given.
//
// What lives here is the FORMAT: the table KIND above, the rosters each instance is seeded with, and the
// bytes they render to.

// The names the table starts with, in wire order. Index 0 is the monitor's "no name" and carries an EMPTY
// entry rather than the `Untagged` case's own spelling — which would otherwise print as a type name for
// an allocation that has none. The rule is the CONSUMER's (`ResolveInternedName` skips index 0), asked
// here rather than restated as a second convention.
export function mmTagRosterNames() returns StringArray
	var names = StringArray.create()
	for tag in MmTag.allCases 'each'
		names.push("" if tag == MmTag.Untagged else tag.name)
	end 'each'
	return names
end 'mmTagRosterNames'

// ⭐ The interned-NAME roster's seed — ONE empty entry, so the first name a program interns is index 1.
// Stated beside the tag roster because it is the same rule read the other way: that one reserves index 0 by
// giving `MmTag.Untagged` an empty spelling, this one by having nothing else at 0 to give.
export function debugStreamNameRosterSeed() returns StringArray
	var names = StringArray.create()
	names.push("")
	return names
end 'debugStreamNameRosterSeed'

// ⭐ One `.symtab` payload: the magic, the count, then each name length-prefixed. Asked TWICE, by
// `installDebugStreamRuntime`, after every interning site has run — once per table, differing only in the
// MAGIC, which is why that is the parameter and the format is not written down twice.
//
// ⚠ **THE FIELDS ARE `writeWord`, WHICH IS ALREADY THE TREE'S ONE 2-BYTE LITTLE-ENDIAN WRITER** (the PE
// writer's section-table and COFF-symbol fields are the same primitive). A second byte-splitting loop here
// would be that fact written twice, and the failure mode of a drift between them is not a build error: it
// would slide the monitor's parse one byte and turn every name after the first into garbage.
function debugStreamNameBlob(magic ByteArray, names StringArray) returns ByteArray
	var blob = ByteArray.create()
	blob.append(magic)
	appendNameBlobField(blob, value: names.count(), what: "the name count")
	for name in names 'each'
		let bytes = name.toByteArray()
		appendNameBlobField(blob, value: bytes.count(), what: "the UTF-8 length of name '{name}'")
		blob.append(bytes)
	end 'each'
	return blob
end 'debugStreamNameBlob'

// ⭐ The tag index for a USER type's box. `Untagged` when this build carries no producer, so an untraced
// build never grows a table it will not render — and the ONE door a dynamic name comes through, so
// "which spelling does a type get in the trace" is answered in a single place.
export function userTagIndex(globalData GlobalDataTable, name String, debugStream bool) returns ParsedInt
	if not debugStream 'untracedBuild'
		return mmTagIndex(MmTag.Untagged)
	end 'untracedBuild'

	return globalData.debugStreamTags.intern(name)
end 'userTagIndex'

// One blob field. BOTH fields in the format — the table's count and each name's length — are the same
// 2-byte little-endian word, so they are written by the same call and cannot come to disagree about width.
//
// ⚠ **AND IT IS THE ONE BOUND CHECK, WHICH IS WHY THE INTERNER HAS NONE.** A count that will not fit is
// exactly a tag index that will not fit, so a second test beside the minter would be the same fact written
// twice. It REFUSES rather than truncating: `writeWord` would silently keep the low two bytes, which does
// not lose one name — it desynchronises the blob and renames every allocation after the offender.
function appendNameBlobField(blob ByteArray, value ElementCount, what String)
	if value > DsNameBlobFieldMax 'doesNotFit'
		panic("DebugStream name blob: {what} is {value}, which does not fit its {DsNameBlobFieldBytes}-byte field (max {DsNameBlobFieldMax})")
	end 'doesNotFit'

	writeWord(blob, value: value)
end 'appendNameBlobField'

// ============================================================================
// The emitted entry points and their `.data` slots.
// ============================================================================

// ⚠ **THE `__ds_` PREFIX IS THE FAMILY'S ONLY MARKING, AND THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO `isDebugStreamRuntimeCallee`
// PREDICATE TO GO WITH IT.** Every other runtime family has one (`isRuntimeCallee`, `isGtRuntimeCallee`,
// `isClockRuntimeCallee`) because a program's SOURCE can reach that family and the usage scan must notice.
// Nothing in a program can ask for a trace — `--debugstream` is the only thing that installs this family,
// and its two entry points are called from the entry stub alone — so a predicate here would be vocabulary
// with no consumer, which is the rule `MmRuntime`'s header opens with. The `__` prefix still does its own
// job: no user name can collide, and `isCompilerInternalCallee` routes past the user-function checks.
//
// ⚠ NOT `__mm_`-prefixed, so `isRuntimeCallee`'s heap test is unaffected — this family allocates nothing
// through the MM layer, and a program that carries it is one the flag already decided about.

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE `__ds_` NAME THAT IS NOT AN ENTRY POINT — the `__DebugStream.nameId("…")` MARKER.** The
// parser emits a zero-argument call to it and records the literal in `Project.debugStreamNameSites`;
// `LowerMaxonToStd` replaces the whole call with the interned index, so no call to this name ever reaches a
// backend and no function is ever built for it. It wears the family prefix because it is the family's, and
// because that is what routes it past the user-function checks (`isCompilerInternalCallee`).
//
// ⚠ **THE NAME EXISTS SO THE FAILURE IS LOUD.** Were the marker some undistinguished shape, a lowering that
// stopped replacing it would emit a call to nothing and die in the linker without a name to blame; as it is,
// `resolveCallFixups` would say `call to unknown function '__ds_name_id'`.
export let DebugStreamNameIdMarkerName = b"__ds_name_id"

// ⭐⭐ **THE FIVE LOG MARKERS — WHAT THE PARSER EMITS WHERE THE SOURCE WROTE A `__DebugStream` MEMBER, AND
// WHAT THE LOWERING EITHER DROPS OR TURNS INTO A CALL.** They exist for `DebugStreamNameIdMarkerName`'s
// reason, applied to the OTHER half of the gating rule: whether a build carries the producer at all is a
// whole-program answer (`buildCarriesDebugStream`), and a parser holds one file. So the parser records the
// SITE and the first tier that walks the merged module decides what it becomes — nothing, or the runtime
// call (`LowerMaxonToStd.lowerDebugStreamEmit`).
//
// ⚠ **THERE ARE FIVE MARKERS AND THREE ENTRY POINTS, AND THE MISMATCH IS THE POINT.** `phase_begin` and
// `phase_end` are one entry taking the wire code as an argument, and `enabled` is no call at all — it
// becomes a load and a compare, or the constant `false`. A marker per SOURCE MEMBER is what lets the
// lowering answer each one differently without re-deriving which member was written.
//
// ⚠ **THEY SHARE THE `__ds_log_` PREFIX, AND THAT PREFIX IS READ** — `isDebugStreamLogMarker`, which is
// how `scanRuntimeUsage` sees that a program emits log events at all. A sixth member added without the
// prefix would compile and would not declare the runtime it needs, so the prefix is the family's marking
// exactly as `__ds_` is the producer's.
let DebugStreamLogMarkerPrefix = b"__ds_log_"
export let DsLogEnabledMarkerName = b"__ds_log_enabled"
export let DsLogPhaseBeginMarkerName = b"__ds_log_phase_begin"
export let DsLogPhaseEndMarkerName = b"__ds_log_phase_end"
export let DsLogEventMarkerName = b"__ds_log_event"
export let DsLogTextMarkerName = b"__ds_log_text"

// ⭐ **DOES THIS CALLEE MARK A `__DebugStream` LOG SITE?** Two askers that must never disagree: the usage
// scan (which turns the producer's transitive needs on) and the lowering (which replaces every one of
// them). Written once for the reason `isBuiltinsIntrinsicCallee` is — a band's membership question belongs
// with the band's names.
export function isDebugStreamLogMarker(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: DebugStreamLogMarkerPrefix)
end 'isDebugStreamLogMarker'

export let DsInitName = b"__ds_init"
export let DsShutdownName = b"__ds_shutdown"
let DsReserveName = b"__ds_reserve"
let DsCommitName = b"__ds_commit"
let DsEmitMmName = b"__ds_emit_mm"

// ⭐ **THE THREE LOG ENTRY POINTS, AND THE ONLY `__ds_` NAMES A LOWERED PROGRAM CALLS DIRECTLY.** Every
// other name in this family is reached from inside it; these are emitted at the site the SOURCE wrote a
// `__DebugStream` member (`LowerMaxonToStd.lowerDebugStreamEmit`), which is why they are exported.
//
// ⚠ **THERE ARE THREE, NOT FOUR — `phaseBegin` AND `phaseEnd` ARE ONE ENTRY.** They differ in one
// immediate, the event type, and the lowering has that immediate in hand; two bodies would be the same
// graph twice.
export let DsEmitLogPhaseName = b"__ds_emit_log_phase"
export let DsEmitLogEventName = b"__ds_emit_log_event"
export let DsEmitLogTextName = b"__ds_emit_log_text"

// The mapped segment's base, or 0 when no monitor is attached. ⭐ **IT IS THE RUN-TIME GATE, AND IT IS
// WRITTEN LAST BY `__ds_init` ON PURPOSE**: every bail on the way through init simply leaves it at its
// `.data` zero, so there is no "un-arm" path to get wrong and a partially-initialised producer cannot
// exist.
export let DsBaseLabel = b"__ds_base"
let DsBufSizeLabel = b"__ds_buf_size"

// `buf_size - 1`, cached so the ring's modulo is one AND.
let DsBufMaskLabel = b"__ds_buf_mask"
let DsStartTsLabel = b"__ds_start_ts"

// A POINTER to the slab-allocated CRITICAL_SECTION, exactly as `SchedRuntime`'s `__sched_lock` is a pointer to
// its own: `InitializeCriticalSection` needs a valid region to initialize in place, and a `.data` slot is
// one machine word.
let DsLockLabel = b"__ds_lock"

// The monotonic allocation id `__mm_alloc` stamps into each box header. Starts at 0 and pre-increments,
// so the first allocation is #1 and a zero packed id can only mean "never stamped".
export let DsAllocIdLabel = b"__mm_alloc_id"

// The bytes `__ds_init` asks `__slab_alloc` for: the CRITICAL_SECTION (5 words, matching `GtRuntime`'s
// own reading of the structure) and the scratch buffer `GetEnvironmentVariableA` writes the segment name into.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THEY ARE NOT THE SAME KIND OF ONE-SHOT, AND THIS NOTE USED TO SAY THEY WERE (S3).** It read *"both
// are one-shot and never reclaimed — the bump allocator has no free list to return them to"*, which is now
// false about the allocator and was always false about one of the two:
//
//   • the CRITICAL_SECTION is LIVE for the whole process, because every event site enters and leaves it until
//     `__ds_shutdown` and there is no later moment at which nothing can be emitting. Never freeing it is not a
//     leak, it is its lifetime — the same reading `__sched_lock`'s region has.
//   • the ENV BUFFER is dead the instant `OpenFileMappingA` has resolved the name into a handle, which happens
//     inside `__ds_init`'s own entry block. It is freed there.
// The monitor names its segments `maxon_ds_<pid>_<8 hex>`; 512 bytes is two orders of magnitude of head
// room and one slab bump.
let DsEnvBufferBytes = 512

// The `.data` slots the producer owns, laid out only for a `--debugstream` build — so every other
// program's `.data` image is unchanged. Ordinary i64 slots in every respect, exactly like MmRuntime's
// `runtimeGlobals()` and GtRuntime's `gtRuntimeGlobals()`.
export function debugStreamRuntimeGlobals() returns DataSectionEntryArray
	var out = DataSectionEntryArray.create()
	// The first five are written by `__ds_init` alone, which the ENTRY STUB runs before `main` and therefore
	// before any green thread, let alone any worker M. The ring itself is not among them: it lives in shared
	// MEMORY and is guarded by the CRITICAL_SECTION `__ds_lock` points at.
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: DsBaseLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: DsBufSizeLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: DsBufMaskLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: DsStartTsLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: DsLockLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: DsAllocIdLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.atomicStep)
	return out
end 'debugStreamRuntimeGlobals'

// The activation environment variable, whose VALUE names the ring segment. Stated ONCE: the monitor sets
// this exact variable, and a drift that left the producer looking at the wrong name would not fail to
// build — the trace would simply never appear.
let DsActivationEnvVar = "MAXON_DEBUGSTREAM"
let DsEnvNameRdataPrefix = "__ds_env_"

// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS BUILD CARRY THE PRODUCER? ONE ANSWER, FOR EVERY PART THAT MUST AGREE.** Four things ride
// this decision and they are in three files: the six `.data` slots, this install, the module flag every
// runtime builder reads, and the entry stub's `__ds_init`/`__ds_shutdown` calls. Spelled per site, the
// conjunction would be one fact written four times — and the failure mode of a drift is not a wrong
// number: a stub that calls an `__ds_init` nothing installed fails to link, and slots laid out for a
// producer that was never built are `.data` a program pays for and never touches.
//
// ⚠ **`usesHeap` IS HALF OF IT, AND NOT A SHORTCUT.** Every event this producer emits is an MM event, so a
// program with no allocator has none to emit — and the init path itself needs `__slab_alloc` for its two
// scratch regions. A `--debugstream` build of a heapless program therefore carries nothing and traces
// nothing, which is the correct answer rather than an omission.
export function buildCarriesDebugStream(project Project, usage RuntimeUsage) returns bool
	return project.debugStream and usage.usesHeap
end 'buildCarriesDebugStream'

// Install the producer into the lowered Std module. Appended AFTER the pipeline like every other runtime
// install, and already-lowered Std so it still flows through the whole backend (what runs over that
// appended band is stated once, in `Compiler.compileToCodeResult`'s install block). Every interning site has finished writing `globalData`'s tag table by the time this runs — the
// blob it renders is what the monitor reads names out of.
//
// ⚠ **`usesGt` IS TAKEN RATHER THAN GUESSED, AND IT DECIDES ONE FIELD.** Every LOG entry stamps the
// emitting green thread, which lives in `P->currentGt` — reached through a `.data` slot only a `usesGt` build lays out.
// A producer that read it unconditionally would put a `globalAddr` for a slot the image does not have into
// every scalar program's trace runtime, and the backend panics at the relocation rather than at the read.
// See `emitStoreLogIdentity`.
export function installDebugStreamRuntime(module StdModule, globalData GlobalDataTable, usesGt bool, rawFree ByteArray)
	let envNameLabel = globalData.registerRdataConstant(DsEnvNameRdataPrefix, bytes: cString(DsActivationEnvVar))
	// ⚠ **BOTH TABLES, END TO END, IN ONE IMAGE.** The monitor scans the section for each magic rather than
	// indexing to a known offset, so their ORDER carries nothing and neither needs a label of its own. The
	// interned-name table is written beside the tag table even when the program interned nothing: it is then
	// the magic plus a zero count — ten bytes that say "this table exists and is empty", which is an answer,
	// where an absent blob is indistinguishable from a compiler that never learned to write one.
	var symdata = debugStreamNameBlob(DsTagBlobMagic, names: globalData.debugStreamTags.names)
	symdata.append(debugStreamNameBlob(DsStrBlobMagic, names: globalData.debugStreamNames.names))
	globalData.symdataImage = symdata

	buildDsInit(module, envNameLabel: envNameLabel.toByteArray(), rawFree: rawFree)
	buildDsShutdown(module)
	buildDsReserve(module)
	buildDsCommit(module)
	buildDsEmitMm(module)
	// ⚠ **THE LOG FAMILY IS BUILT UNCONDITIONALLY WITH THE REST, NOT ONLY WHEN A PROGRAM EMITS ONE.** Its
	// three entry points are dead code in a traced program that writes no `__DebugStream` member, and
	// `deadFunctions` is the pass that answers that question — for this family exactly as it does for
	// `__ds_emit_mm`, whose callers are equally conditional. Gating the BUILD on a usage bit would be a
	// second answer to a question one pass already owns.
	buildDsEmitLogPhase(module, usesGt: usesGt)
	buildDsEmitLogEvent(module, usesGt: usesGt)
	buildDsEmitLogText(module, usesGt: usesGt)
end 'installDebugStreamRuntime'

// A NUL-terminated C string's bytes. `GetEnvironmentVariableA` and `OpenFileMappingA` both take one, and
// the terminator is the whole difference from `String.toByteArray()`.
function cString(text String) returns ByteArray
	var bytes = text.toByteArray()
	bytes.push(0)
	return bytes
end 'cString'

// ============================================================================
// `__ds_init()` — attach, or stay dark.
// ============================================================================
//
// ⭐ **THE HANDSHAKE IS TWO VERSIONS TRAVELLING IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS, AND THE ORDER OF THE TWO STEPS IS
// ARGUED RATHER THAN INCIDENTAL.** This producer ANNOUNCES its schema (`DsOffProducerVersion`) BEFORE it
// checks the monitor's (`DsOffVersion`), because a producer that refuses an incompatible monitor and says
// nothing is indistinguishable, to that monitor, from a binary built without `--debugstream` at all: both
// are an empty ring. Announcing first means a reader of any version can always see what this binary
// speaks, so the mismatch is loud from whichever side notices it. It is safe to write there and nowhere
// earlier because the magic check above is what established that this segment is a DebugStream ring and
// not somebody else's shared memory.
//
// ⚠ **A MISMATCHED MONITOR GETS SILENCE, NOT AN APPROXIMATION.** A v1 monitor treats "below write_cursor"
// as readable, so writing v2 entries into it hands it our UNCOMMITTED payloads — reintroducing, silently,
// the exact torn read the commit bit closes. An empty trace is a visibly missing instrument; a wrong one
// is not.
function buildDsInit(module StdModule, envNameLabel ByteArray, rawFree ByteArray)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(DsInitName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var checkMagic = module.addBlock(func, label: b"checkMagic")
	var checkVersion = module.addBlock(func, label: b"checkVersion")
	var attach = module.addBlock(func, label: b"attach")
	var dark = module.addBlock(func, label: b"dark")

	// The two scratch regions. `__slab_alloc` hands back ZEROED memory (the allocator's zeroing contract),
	// which is what lets the env lookup below need no "was it set?" branch: an absent variable leaves the
	// buffer naming the EMPTY string, and `OpenFileMappingA("")` fails exactly as a missing segment does. The
	// lock outlives this function; the env buffer does not — see `CriticalSectionBytes`.
	let lockSize = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: CriticalSectionBytes, ids: ids)
	let lock = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(lock, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(lockSize)))
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osLockInit(lock))
	let lockAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(lockAddr, label: DsLockLabel))
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(lockAddr, offset: 0, valueId: lock, valueType: StdType.i64))

	let envBufSize = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: DsEnvBufferBytes, ids: ids)
	let envBuf = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(envBuf, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(envBufSize)))
	let envName = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.rdataAddr(envName, label: envNameLabel))
	let handle = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osOpenNamedSegment(handle, namePtrId: envName, bufPtrId: envBuf, bufLenId: envBufSize))
	let segmentBytes = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: DsSharedMemoryBytes, ids: ids)
	let base = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osMapNamedSegment(base, handleId: handle, sizeId: segmentBytes))
	// The section handle has done its job the moment the view exists; the VIEW keeps the pages alive.
	// Closing a NULL handle is a harmless FALSE, which is why the failed-open path needs no arm here. The name
	// buffer is spent for the same reason — the open resolved it — and it is released here rather than on each
	// of the four exits below, all of which pass through this block.
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(handle))
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: rawFree, args: argsOf(envBuf)))
	let mapped = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: base, b: emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(mapped, thenBlockId: checkMagic.id, elseBlockId: dark.id))

	// `base` is defined in `entry`, which dominates every block below it, so it is read directly rather
	// than threaded as a block argument — ordinary SSA.
	let magic = emitLoadField(module, block: checkMagic, record: base, offset: DsOffMagic, ids: ids)
	let expectedMagic = emitConst(module, block: checkMagic, value: DsMagic, ids: ids)
	let magicOk = emitCmp(module, block: checkMagic, a: magic, b: expectedMagic, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkMagic, op: StdOp.condBranch(magicOk, thenBlockId: checkVersion.id, elseBlockId: dark.id))

	let ourVersion = emitConst(module, block: checkVersion, value: DsSchemaVersion, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(checkVersion, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(base, offset: DsOffProducerVersion, valueId: ourVersion, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let monitorVersion = emitLoadField(module, block: checkVersion, record: base, offset: DsOffVersion, ids: ids)
	let versionOk = emitCmp(module, block: checkVersion, a: monitorVersion, b: ourVersion, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkVersion, op: StdOp.condBranch(versionOk, thenBlockId: attach.id, elseBlockId: dark.id))

	let bufSize = emitLoadField(module, block: attach, record: base, offset: DsOffBufferSize, ids: ids)
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: attach, label: DsBufSizeLabel, value: bufSize, ids: ids)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: attach, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let bufMask = emitBin(module, block: attach, a: bufSize, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: attach, label: DsBufMaskLabel, value: bufMask, ids: ids)
	let startTs = emitLoadField(module, block: attach, record: base, offset: DsOffStartTimestamp, ids: ids)
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: attach, label: DsStartTsLabel, value: startTs, ids: ids)
	let alive = emitConst(module, block: attach, value: DsFlagProducerAlive, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(attach, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(base, offset: DsOffFlags, valueId: alive, valueType: StdType.i64))
	// ⭐ ARMED LAST. Every store above must be in place before any event site's `__ds_base != 0` test can
	// succeed, and this is the store that makes it succeed.
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: attach, label: DsBaseLabel, value: base, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(attach, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	// Nothing to un-do: `__ds_base` is still its `.data` zero, so the producer is dark by construction.
	module.setTerminator(dark, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildDsInit'

// `__ds_shutdown()` — clear `producer_alive`.
//
// ⚠ It does NOT clear the announced version, and does not unmap. The version is a fact about the BINARY
// rather than about its liveness, and a monitor draining the ring of a producer that has already exited
// still needs to know how to parse what it left. The view is dropped by process exit, which is the only
// thing that happens after this call.
function buildDsShutdown(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(DsShutdownName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var clear = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clear")
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	let base = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: DsBaseLabel, ids: ids)
	let attached = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: base, b: emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(attached, thenBlockId: clear.id, elseBlockId: done.id))

	let zero = emitConst(module, block: clear, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(clear, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(base, offset: DsOffFlags, valueId: zero, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(clear, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildDsShutdown'

// `[base + offset] += 1` — the two ring counters the monitor's closing summary reads (`total_events`,
// `dropped_events`). Both increments happen under the ring lock, which is why a plain read-modify-write is
// the whole of it: this is `MmRuntime`'s own argument for its unlocked `__mm_alloc_count`, one level
// stronger, because here the mutual exclusion is explicit rather than a property of the rung.
function emitBumpSegmentCounter(module StdModule, block IrBlock, base ValueId, offset ByteOffset, ids ValueMinter)
	let current = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: base, offset: offset, ids: ids)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let raised = emitBin(module, block: block, a: current, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(base, offset: offset, valueId: raised, valueType: StdType.i64))
end 'emitBumpSegmentCounter'

// The address of ring offset `pos`: `base + DsHeaderBytes + pos`. The ring's bytes start immediately after
// the 128-byte segment header, and that relationship is spelled here rather than at each of the two
// writers — a padding entry and a real one address the ring the same way.
function emitRingSlotAddr(module StdModule, block IrBlock, base ValueId, pos ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let headerBytes = emitConst(module, block: block, value: DsHeaderBytes, ids: ids)
	let ringBase = emitBin(module, block: block, a: base, b: headerBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: ringBase, b: pos, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
end 'emitRingSlotAddr'

// `value << shift` — one field of a packed word, positioned. Four sites pack a field into an entry header
// or an mm payload and every one of them is this.
function emitShiftedField(module StdModule, block IrBlock, value ValueId, shift ParsedInt, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let amount = emitConst(module, block: block, value: shift, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: value, b: amount, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
end 'emitShiftedField'

// `write_cursor & buf_mask` — the ring OFFSET the absolute cursor names. The mask is `buf_size - 1` and
// the buffer is a power of two, which is what makes the modulo one AND.
function emitRingPos(module StdModule, block IrBlock, base ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let cursor = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: base, offset: DsOffWriteCursor, ids: ids)
	let mask = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: DsBufMaskLabel, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: cursor, b: mask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
end 'emitRingPos'

// `(write_cursor - read_cursor) + entrySize <= buf_size` — is there room? Asked TWICE by `__ds_reserve`
// (before and after a wrap's padding), off freshly re-read cursors both times, so the two cannot come to
// disagree about what "room" means. `fits` and `dropped` are the caller's blocks.
function emitSpaceCheck(module StdModule, block IrBlock, base ValueId, entrySize ValueId, fits IrBlock, dropped IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let cursor = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: base, offset: DsOffWriteCursor, ids: ids)
	let reader = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: base, offset: DsOffReadCursor, ids: ids)
	let used = emitBin(module, block: block, a: cursor, b: reader, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let needed = emitBin(module, block: block, a: used, b: entrySize, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let bufSize = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: DsBufSizeLabel, ids: ids)
	let roomy = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: needed, b: bufSize, pred: StdCmpPred.le, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.condBranch(roomy, thenBlockId: fits.id, elseBlockId: dropped.id))
end 'emitSpaceCheck'

// ============================================================================
// `__ds_reserve(eventType, entrySize) -> entry pointer, or 0 when the ring is full`
// ============================================================================
//
// ⭐ **THE ENTRY IS NOT READABLE WHEN THIS RETURNS.** The header goes down with the commit bit CLEAR, the
// cursor advances and the ring lock is released — all before the caller has written a byte of payload.
// Publishing the cursor is what makes the entry VISIBLE; setting the commit bit, in `__ds_commit`, is what
// makes it READABLE. The two are deliberately different moments, because the payload must be written
// outside the lock.
//
// ⚠ **THERE IS NO DETACHED PATH HERE, AND THAT IS THE GATING RULE DOING ITS JOB.** The caller has already
// tested `__ds_base` inline (see `emitMmEvent`), so a `--debugstream` binary with no monitor attached
// never reaches this function and never pays a call. The reference's `__ds_reserve` re-asks that question
// after two CALLs have already happened; this one does not have to.
//
// ⚠ **EVERY BLOCK BELOW RE-READS `write_cursor` FROM THE SEGMENT HEADER RATHER THAN THREADING IT.** That
// is not a workaround for the Std tier having no block arguments — it is CORRECT, for the reason their
// absence is survivable here: the whole function runs under the ring lock, so the header word cannot
// change underneath it, and a re-read and a thread are the same value by construction. It is also what
// lets the padding path publish its own advance through the ordinary cursor store instead of a second one.
function buildDsReserve(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(DsReserveName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var raisePeak = module.addBlock(func, label: b"raisePeak")
	var checkSpace = module.addBlock(func, label: b"checkSpace")
	var checkWrap = module.addBlock(func, label: b"checkWrap")
	var pad = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pad")
	var recheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"recheck")
	var place = module.addBlock(func, label: b"place")
	var full = module.addBlock(func, label: b"full")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let eventType = 0 as ValueId
	let entrySize = 1 as ValueId

	let base = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: DsBaseLabel, ids: ids)
	let lock = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: DsLockLabel, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osLockEnter(lock))

	// PEAK first, because it is a fact about how full the ring GOT rather than about this entry: an event
	// that is about to be dropped still pushed the high-water mark to where it is.
	let cursor = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: base, offset: DsOffWriteCursor, ids: ids)
	let reader = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: base, offset: DsOffReadCursor, ids: ids)
	let used = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: cursor, b: reader, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let peak = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: base, offset: DsOffPeakUsed, ids: ids)
	let higher = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: used, b: peak, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(higher, thenBlockId: raisePeak.id, elseBlockId: checkSpace.id))

	module.appendOp(raisePeak, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(base, offset: DsOffPeakUsed, valueId: used, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(raisePeak, op: StdOp.branch(checkSpace.id))

	emitSpaceCheck(module, block: checkSpace, base: base, entrySize: entrySize, fits: checkWrap, dropped: full, ids: ids)

	// Would this entry straddle the ring's wrap? `pos = cursor & mask`; if `pos + entry_size` runs past the
	// ring's end, the tail is filled with a padding entry and the real one starts at ring offset 0.
	let bufSize = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: checkWrap, label: DsBufSizeLabel, ids: ids)
	let pos = emitRingPos(module, block: checkWrap, base: base, ids: ids)
	let posEnd = emitBin(module, block: checkWrap, a: pos, b: entrySize, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let noWrap = emitCmp(module, block: checkWrap, a: posEnd, b: bufSize, pred: StdCmpPred.le, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkWrap, op: StdOp.condBranch(noWrap, thenBlockId: place.id, elseBlockId: pad.id))

	// ⭐ THE PADDING ENTRY IS BORN COMMITTED — see `DsPaddingHeaderTypeAndFlags`. Its size is the distance
	// to the end of the ring, so the entry after it starts at ring offset 0.
	let padBufSize = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: pad, label: DsBufSizeLabel, ids: ids)
	let padPos = emitRingPos(module, block: pad, base: base, ids: ids)
	let padSize = emitBin(module, block: pad, a: padBufSize, b: padPos, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let padAddr = emitRingSlotAddr(module, block: pad, base: base, pos: padPos, ids: ids)
	let padSizeField = emitShiftedField(module, block: pad, value: padSize, shift: DsEntrySizeShift, ids: ids)
	let padTypeAndFlags = emitConst(module, block: pad, value: DsPaddingHeaderTypeAndFlags, ids: ids)
	let padHeader = emitBin(module, block: pad, a: padSizeField, b: padTypeAndFlags, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(pad, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(padAddr, offset: 0, valueId: padHeader, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let padCursor = emitLoadField(module, block: pad, record: base, offset: DsOffWriteCursor, ids: ids)
	let paddedCursor = emitBin(module, block: pad, a: padCursor, b: padSize, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(pad, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(base, offset: DsOffWriteCursor, valueId: paddedCursor, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(pad, op: StdOp.branch(recheck.id))

	// ⚠ The space check is asked AGAIN after padding, and it is not the same question: the padding entry
	// consumed ring bytes of its own, so a reservation that fitted before it may not fit after. Skipping
	// this is how a producer overwrites entries the monitor has not read.
	emitSpaceCheck(module, block: recheck, base: base, entrySize: entrySize, fits: place, dropped: full, ids: ids)

	// PLACE: write the entry header UNCOMMITTED, then publish it by advancing the cursor.
	let placeCursor = emitLoadField(module, block: place, record: base, offset: DsOffWriteCursor, ids: ids)
	let placePos = emitRingPos(module, block: place, base: base, ids: ids)
	let dataPtr = emitRingSlotAddr(module, block: place, base: base, pos: placePos, ids: ids)

	let now = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(place, op: StdOp.osTickCountMs(now))
	let startTs = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: place, label: DsStartTsLabel, ids: ids)
	let delta = emitBin(module, block: place, a: now, b: startTs, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)

	// ⭐ `flags` IS ZERO AND THAT IS NOT FILLER — it is the entry's UNCOMMITTED state, and it falls out for
	// free because `event_type` is a byte and `entry_size` starts at bit 16.
	let sizeField = emitShiftedField(module, block: place, value: entrySize, shift: DsEntrySizeShift, ids: ids)
	let timeField = emitShiftedField(module, block: place, value: delta, shift: DsEntryTimestampShift, ids: ids)
	let typeAndSize = emitBin(module, block: place, a: eventType, b: sizeField, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	let header = emitBin(module, block: place, a: typeAndSize, b: timeField, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(place, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(dataPtr, offset: 0, valueId: header, valueType: StdType.i64))

	// ⭐ THE RING'S ONE PUBLISH POINT. It orders every store above it — this entry's header, and any
	// padding entry written on the way — ahead of the cursor that makes them reachable. On x64 a plain
	// store IS a release store (TSO), which is what makes this correct with no barrier op at this tier; a
	// target whose stores may be reordered would owe one, and `--debugstream` is refused on every such
	// target today.
	let published = emitBin(module, block: place, a: placeCursor, b: entrySize, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(place, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(base, offset: DsOffWriteCursor, valueId: published, valueType: StdType.i64))
	emitBumpSegmentCounter(module, block: place, base: base, offset: DsOffTotalEvents, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(place, op: StdOp.osLockLeave(lock))
	module.setTerminator(place, op: StdOp.ret(dataPtr))

	// DROPPED. Both space checks reach here, and both reach it HOLDING the lock — which is why the release
	// follows from the CONTROL PATH rather than from a re-derived test. The reference records that getting
	// this wrong is not a lost event but a HANG: every later producer waits on a lock nobody released.
	emitBumpSegmentCounter(module, block: full, base: base, offset: DsOffDroppedEvents, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(full, op: StdOp.osLockLeave(lock))
	emitConstReturn(module, block: full, value: 0, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildDsReserve'

// ============================================================================
// `__ds_commit(entryPtr)` — the entry is now READABLE.
// ============================================================================
//
// The read-modify-write of the header word needs no atomic: `__ds_reserve` handed this entry to exactly
// one caller, that caller is the only thing that ever writes the entry again, and the monitor only reads
// it — so nothing can race the OR.
//
// What it DOES need is release ordering, so the payload writes are visible to the monitor before the bit
// that claims they are there. On x64 that is a plain store; see `__ds_reserve`'s publish point for why
// this tier has no barrier op and what would owe one.
function buildDsCommit(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(DsCommitName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let entryPtr = 0 as ValueId

	let header = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: entryPtr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let bit = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: DsEntryCommittedBit, ids: ids)
	let committed = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: header, b: bit, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(entryPtr, offset: 0, valueId: committed, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildDsCommit'

// ============================================================================
// `__ds_emit_mm(eventType, packedId, value)` — the ONE mm event body.
// ============================================================================
//
// ⭐ **ONE EMITTER FOR ALL FOUR MM EVENTS, BECAUSE THEY ARE ONE ENTRY SHAPE.** `mm_alloc`, `mm_free`,
// `mm_incref` and `mm_decref` are 24 bytes each, carrying an alloc id, a tag index and a 32-bit value — an
// allocation SIZE for the alloc, the new OWNER COUNT for the refcount pair, and unused (0) for the free.
// The reference splits them into three functions sharing one payload writer; splitting them here would be
// three bodies differing in one immediate.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS THE ONLY CALLER OF `__ds_reserve`, AND IT ALWAYS COMMITS.** A producer therefore cannot
// publish an entry and forget to commit it: the pairing is the only shape a call site can have. A missed
// commit does not lose one event — an uncommitted entry is never skipped while its producer lives, so the
// monitor STALLS ON IT FOREVER.
function buildDsEmitMm(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(DsEmitMmName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 3, paramTypes: threeI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(3)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var write = module.addBlock(func, label: b"write")
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 3)
	let eventType = 0 as ValueId
	let packedId = 1 as ValueId
	let value = 2 as ValueId

	let entryBytes = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: DsMmEntryBytes, ids: ids)
	let entryPtr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(entryPtr, callee: DsReserveName, args: twoArgs(eventType, second: entryBytes)))
	// 0 means the ring was FULL. The entry was never reserved, so there is nothing to commit — this is a
	// DROPPED event, counted in the segment header, not a deferred one.
	let noEntry = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let reserved = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: entryPtr, b: noEntry, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(reserved, thenBlockId: write.id, elseBlockId: done.id))

	// The payload, written OUTSIDE the ring lock — which is the whole reason the commit bit exists.
	let shift = emitConst(module, block: write, value: DsMmPackedIdShift, ids: ids)
	let allocId = emitBin(module, block: write, a: packedId, b: shift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(write, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(entryPtr, offset: DsMmOffAllocId, valueId: allocId, valueType: StdType.i64))

	let tagIndex = emitMaskedField(module, block: write, value: packedId, mask: DsMmTagIndexMask, ids: ids)
	// `scope_len` (bits 16..31) stays zero: this producer emits no scope names, so the slot has nothing to
	// carry and the monitor's mm formatter never reads it.
	let valueField = emitShiftedField(module, block: write, value: value, shift: DsMmValueShift, ids: ids)
	let packed = emitBin(module, block: write, a: tagIndex, b: valueField, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(write, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(entryPtr, offset: DsMmOffPacked, valueId: packed, valueType: StdType.i64))

	let committed = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(write, op: StdOp.call(committed, callee: DsCommitName, args: argsOf(entryPtr)))
	module.setTerminator(write, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildDsEmitMm'

// ============================================================================
// The LOG family's three bodies — `__ds_emit_log_phase` / `_event` / `_text`.
// ============================================================================
//
// ⚠⚠ **THESE THREE TEST `__ds_base` THEMSELVES, WHERE `emitMmEvent` TESTS IT AT THE CALL SITE, AND THE
// DIFFERENCE IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN A RELAXED RULE.** The mm family's call sites are built by a RUNTIME
// BUILDER, which owns its blocks and can split one; a LOG event's call site is in USER CODE, and
// `lowerMaxonToStd` maps one Maxon block to one Std block by construction (`stdModule.blocks` is 1:1 with
// `maxonModule.blocks`), so no lowering arm can introduce a branch. The only tier that can is a Std pass
// with block-splitting machinery, and there is exactly one of those (`InsertRangeChecks`). ⇒ a
// `--debugstream` binary with no monitor attached pays ONE call per log site rather than a load and a
// not-taken branch. That is the cost of the position, and it is bounded: a source that wants the inline
// bail has `__DebugStream.enabled()`, which lowers to that load and compare with no call at all — which is
// exactly what `Logger.log` guards its text tier with.
//
// The compile-time half of the gating rule is unaffected and is where it always was: a build that does not
// carry the producer emits none of these calls at all (`LowerMaxonToStd.lowerDebugStreamEmit`).

// ⭐⭐ **EVERY LOG ENTRY CARRIES THE EMITTING GREEN THREAD AND ITS PROCESSOR — THE FAMILY'S WHOLE POINT.**
// It is what lets N interleaved emitters be put back into per-emitter timelines instead of a shuffled
// pile. Read HERE rather than passed in from the call site, so a site costs only its own arguments and
// there is one place to get it right.
//
// ⚠ **`p_id` IS ALWAYS ZERO, AND THAT IS NOW A CHOICE RATHER THAN AN ABSENCE.** The reference schedules
// green threads across N processors and stamps which one ran the event; shv2 HAS a `P` since G1 (its id is
// `SchedRuntime.POffId`) but runs one M by default, so every event would carry the same 0 and the field would
// cost a TLS read per entry to say nothing. It becomes worth stamping the day more than one M runs by
// default, which is the same day it stops being constant. `unit_id` is what shv2's own emitters demux by, and
// it is carried on every one of these entries.
//
// ⚠ **THE CURRENT GT IS READ ONLY WHEN THIS BUILD CARRIES THE SCHEDULER.** The read goes through the P
// (`SchedRuntime`), whose `.data` word is `usesGt`'s (`Compiler.maxon`'s `gtGlobals` arm), so naming it in a
// scalar program would name a slot the image does not have and the backend would panic at the relocation —
// the trap `emitMmEvent`'s header records for `__ds_base` in an untraced build, one family over. A scalar
// program HAS no green thread, so zero is again the true answer rather than a stand-in.
//
// ⛔⛔ **AND IT IS THE `OrZero` READ, NOT THE PLAIN ONE, BECAUSE THIS IS THE ONE READER THAT CANNOT PROMISE
// THE SCHEDULER EXISTS.** Every reader inside `GtRuntime` is downstream of a lazy `__gt_init`; a log event is
// not — a program may log before its first `async`, and the tracing harness is exactly the situation in which
// it does. MEASURED when this used the plain read: a `--debugstream` green-thread program that logs one event
// before its first `async`, under `maxon monitor`, went from exit 42 to a SEGMENTATION FAULT, because the
// uninitialized TEB offset addresses `NT_TIB.ExceptionList` rather than a null TLS slot. The `.data` word this
// replaced could not fail that way, which is why the discipline had never had to be written down.
//
// It returns the block to continue in, because the guarded read branches.
function emitStoreLogIdentity(module StdModule, func IrFunction, block IrBlock, entryPtr ValueId, usesGt bool, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var cur = block
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	var gt = zero
	if usesGt 'scheduled'
		let liveGt = ids.mint()
		cur = emitLoadCurrentGtOrZero(module, func: func, cur: cur, gtId: liveGt, ids: ids)
		gt = liveGt
	end 'scheduled'

	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(entryPtr, offset: DsLogOffGt, valueId: gt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	// ⚠ The zero the PID field stores is minted in `block`, which dominates `cur` on every arm above, so the
	// branch the guarded read introduces cannot strand it.
	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(entryPtr, offset: DsLogOffPid, valueId: zero, valueType: StdType.i64))
	return cur
end 'emitStoreLogIdentity'

// `value & mask` — one field of a packed word, narrowed to its wire width. `emitShiftedField`'s sibling,
// and read by BOTH families: five sites narrow a field before positioning it (the mm entry's tag index and
// the four log fields), and every one of them is this.
function emitMaskedField(module StdModule, block IrBlock, value ValueId, mask ParsedInt, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let maskConst = emitConst(module, block: block, value: mask, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: value, b: maskConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
end 'emitMaskedField'

// `cat(1) | lvl(1) | u16(2) | unit_id(4)` — the `DsLogOffFields` word LOG_EVENT and LOG_TEXT share. They
// pack it identically and differ only in what the 16-bit slot MEANS, so it is packed once; LOG_PHASE's word
// is a DIFFERENT layout and is packed in its own body (see the constants above for the measured reason).
function emitPackedLogFields(module StdModule, block IrBlock, category ValueId, level ValueId, u16Field ValueId, unitId ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let categoryByte = emitMaskedField(module, block: block, value: category, mask: DsLogCatMask, ids: ids)
	let levelByte = emitMaskedField(module, block: block, value: level, mask: DsLogLvlMask, ids: ids)
	let levelField = emitShiftedField(module, block: block, value: levelByte, shift: DsLogLvlShift, ids: ids)
	let slot = emitMaskedField(module, block: block, value: u16Field, mask: DsLogU16FieldMask, ids: ids)
	let slotField = emitShiftedField(module, block: block, value: slot, shift: DsLogU16FieldShift, ids: ids)
	let unitField = emitShiftedField(module, block: block, value: unitId, shift: DsLogUnitIdShift, ids: ids)

	let bytes = emitBin(module, block: block, a: categoryByte, b: levelField, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	let withSlot = emitBin(module, block: block, a: bytes, b: slotField, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: withSlot, b: unitField, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
end 'emitPackedLogFields'

// ⭐ **THE OPENING EVERY LOG BODY SHARES: BAIL IF DETACHED, RESERVE, BAIL IF THE RING IS FULL.** It closes
// the caller's ENTRY block, mints the `reserve` block between it and the caller's own two, and answers the
// reserved entry's address — which is defined in `reserve` and therefore readable anywhere the caller
// writes below it.
//
// A zero from `__ds_reserve` means the ring was FULL, so the event is DROPPED — counted in the segment
// header, never deferred — and there is nothing to commit.
//
// ⚠ `write` and `done` are the CALLER's blocks rather than this function's answers, because a block must
// be a `var` to be appended to and a field read is not one. It also keeps the caller's `done` terminator
// where the caller can see it.
function emitLogEntryOpen(module StdModule, func IrFunction, entry IrBlock, eventType ValueId, entryBytes ValueId, write IrBlock, done IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	var reserve = module.addBlock(func, label: b"reserve")

	let base = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: DsBaseLabel, ids: ids)
	let detached = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let attached = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: base, b: detached, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(attached, thenBlockId: reserve.id, elseBlockId: done.id))

	let entryPtr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(reserve, op: StdOp.call(entryPtr, callee: DsReserveName, args: twoArgs(eventType, second: entryBytes)))
	let noEntry = emitConst(module, block: reserve, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let reserved = emitCmp(module, block: reserve, a: entryPtr, b: noEntry, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(reserve, op: StdOp.condBranch(reserved, thenBlockId: write.id, elseBlockId: done.id))

	return entryPtr
end 'emitLogEntryOpen'

// The commit every log body ends with, and the branch to its DONE block. Stated once for `buildDsEmitMm`'s
// reason: the reserve and the commit are paired by the SHAPE of a call site rather than by a convention
// each body is trusted to follow, and a missed commit does not lose one event — the monitor never steps
// past an uncommitted entry, so it STALLS on it for as long as the producer lives.
function emitCommitAndFinish(module StdModule, block IrBlock, entryPtr ValueId, done IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let committed = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(committed, callee: DsCommitName, args: argsOf(entryPtr)))
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))
end 'emitCommitAndFinish'

// ============================================================================
// `__ds_emit_log_phase(eventType, nameId, unitId)` — LOG_PHASE_BEGIN / LOG_PHASE_END
// ============================================================================
//
// ONE body for both codes: they differ in the immediate the caller passes and in nothing else, which is
// why the event type is a PARAMETER here where the other two bodies mint their own constant.
//
// The word is `phase_id(2) rsvd(2) unit_id(4)`, and `rsvd` stays zero for free — the masked `phase_id`
// occupies only bits [0:15] and the unit's shift discards anything above bit 31.
function buildDsEmitLogPhase(module StdModule, usesGt bool)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(DsEmitLogPhaseName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 3, paramTypes: threeI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(3)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 3)
	let eventType = 0 as ValueId
	let nameId = 1 as ValueId
	let unitId = 2 as ValueId

	var write = module.addBlock(func, label: b"write")
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	let entryBytes = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: DsLogPhaseEntryBytes, ids: ids)
	let entryPtr = emitLogEntryOpen(module, func: func, entry: entry, eventType: eventType, entryBytes: entryBytes, write: write, done: done, ids: ids)

	write = emitStoreLogIdentity(module, func: func, block: write, entryPtr: entryPtr, usesGt: usesGt, ids: ids)
	let phaseId = emitMaskedField(module, block: write, value: nameId, mask: DsLogU16FieldMask, ids: ids)
	let unitField = emitShiftedField(module, block: write, value: unitId, shift: DsLogUnitIdShift, ids: ids)
	let fields = emitBin(module, block: write, a: phaseId, b: unitField, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(write, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(entryPtr, offset: DsLogOffFields, valueId: fields, valueType: StdType.i64))
	emitCommitAndFinish(module, block: write, entryPtr: entryPtr, done: done, ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildDsEmitLogPhase'

// ============================================================================
// `__ds_emit_log_event(nameId, cat, lvl, unitId, arg0, arg1)` — LOG_EVENT
// ============================================================================
//
// The structured, ZERO-ALLOC tier a compiler pass uses. Its two argument slots are deliberately opaque:
// what they mean is the emitting site's business, and the monitor prints them as numbers.
function buildDsEmitLogEvent(module StdModule, usesGt bool)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(DsEmitLogEventName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 6, paramTypes: sixI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(6)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 6)
	let nameId = 0 as ValueId
	let category = 1 as ValueId
	let level = 2 as ValueId
	let unitId = 3 as ValueId
	let arg0 = 4 as ValueId
	let arg1 = 5 as ValueId

	var write = module.addBlock(func, label: b"write")
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	let eventType = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: DsEvLogEvent, ids: ids)
	let entryBytes = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: DsLogEventEntryBytes, ids: ids)
	let entryPtr = emitLogEntryOpen(module, func: func, entry: entry, eventType: eventType, entryBytes: entryBytes, write: write, done: done, ids: ids)

	write = emitStoreLogIdentity(module, func: func, block: write, entryPtr: entryPtr, usesGt: usesGt, ids: ids)
	let fields = emitPackedLogFields(module, block: write, category: category, level: level, u16Field: nameId, unitId: unitId, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(write, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(entryPtr, offset: DsLogOffFields, valueId: fields, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(write, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(entryPtr, offset: DsLogOffArg0, valueId: arg0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(write, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(entryPtr, offset: DsLogOffArg1, valueId: arg1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	emitCommitAndFinish(module, block: write, entryPtr: entryPtr, done: done, ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildDsEmitLogEvent'

// ============================================================================
// `__ds_emit_log_text(cat, lvl, unitId, record)` — LOG_TEXT
// ============================================================================
//
// ⭐ **IT TAKES THE RECORD, NOT A `(ptr, len)` PAIR, AND THAT IS A DIVERGENCE WITH A REASON.** The
// reference's lowering loads `buffer` and `length` out of the `__ManagedMemory` and passes both; reading
// them here instead keeps the record read in ONE place beside `__write_stdout`'s — `buffer@0` and
// `length@8`, the same two slots at the same two offsets — rather than in a lowering arm that would then
// have to know the layout. One argument fewer, and one fewer place for the offsets to be written down.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE CLAMP IS ARITHMETIC RATHER THAN A BRANCH BECAUSE THE STD TIER HAS NO BLOCK ARGUMENTS.** A
// length that differs per path cannot be MERGED at this tier — `StdOp.branch` carries no operands — so it
// is COMPUTED: `over` is the 0/1 a compare materializes (the domain `setccReg` guarantees, which is what
// makes it a legal multiplicand), and `len = raw - over * (raw - max)` is `raw` when it fits and `max` when
// it does not. The alternative would be a second function whose RETURN REGISTER performed the merge — a
// call per text event to avoid three arithmetic ops.
//
// ⚠ **THE SAME CLAMPED LENGTH FEEDS BOTH THE HEADER'S `len` FIELD AND THE ENTRY SIZE.** Computing one from
// the raw length and the other from the clamped one is how a reader walks off the end of an entry.
function buildDsEmitLogText(module StdModule, usesGt bool)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(DsEmitLogTextName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 4, paramTypes: fourI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(4)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 4)
	let category = 0 as ValueId
	let level = 1 as ValueId
	let unitId = 2 as ValueId
	let record = 3 as ValueId

	var write = module.addBlock(func, label: b"write")
	var zeroPad = module.addBlock(func, label: b"zeroPad")
	var copy = module.addBlock(func, label: b"copy")
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	// Sized in the ENTRY block because `emitLogEntryOpen` needs the size to reserve with. A detached build
	// pays these arithmetic ops and no call, which is the same order of cost its `__ds_base` test is.
	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: record, offset: StringBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let rawLength = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: record, offset: StringLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let maxLength = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: DsLogTextMaxBytes, ids: ids)
	let over = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: rawLength, b: maxLength, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	let excess = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: rawLength, b: maxLength, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let trimmed = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: over, b: excess, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let length = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: rawLength, b: trimmed, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)

	// `align8(len)` — the bias is what makes the shift pair round UP. ⚠ It is a shift pair for no reason that
	// survives inspection: `DsEntryAlign`'s header records what W215's review found, which is that
	// `MmRuntime.emitAlignUp` says this in one op fewer and the excuse for not calling it was never true.
	let bias = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: DsEntryAlignBias, ids: ids)
	let biased = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: length, b: bias, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let alignShift = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: DsEntryAlignShift, ids: ids)
	let units = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: biased, b: alignShift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, ids: ids)
	let tail = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: units, b: alignShift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
	let fixedBytes = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: DsLogTextFixedBytes, ids: ids)
	let entryBytes = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: fixedBytes, b: tail, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)

	let eventType = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: DsEvLogText, ids: ids)
	let entryPtr = emitLogEntryOpen(module, func: func, entry: entry, eventType: eventType, entryBytes: entryBytes, write: write, done: done, ids: ids)

	write = emitStoreLogIdentity(module, func: func, block: write, entryPtr: entryPtr, usesGt: usesGt, ids: ids)
	let fields = emitPackedLogFields(module, block: write, category: category, level: level, u16Field: length, unitId: unitId, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(write, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(entryPtr, offset: DsLogOffFields, valueId: fields, valueType: StdType.i64))

	let textOffset = emitConst(module, block: write, value: DsLogOffText, ids: ids)
	let textBase = emitBin(module, block: write, a: entryPtr, b: textOffset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	// ⚠ **AN EMPTY MESSAGE HAS NO TAIL, AND WITHOUT THIS TEST THE PAD STORE WOULD LAND ON THE FIELDS WORD.**
	// `align8(0)` is 0, so `textBase + tail - 8` is `entryPtr + 24` — the word just written — and a message
	// of length 0 would erase its own category, level and unit. It is the reference's own `JumpIfZero`
	// guard, kept for a reason its comment does not state.
	let noTail = emitConst(module, block: write, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let hasTail = emitCmp(module, block: write, a: tail, b: noTail, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(write, op: StdOp.condBranch(hasTail, thenBlockId: zeroPad.id, elseBlockId: copy.id))

	// ⭐ **THE RING IS REUSED MEMORY, so the pad bytes `[len, align8(len))` would otherwise carry a previous
	// entry's payload** and the monitor would print it after the message. Only the LAST qword of the tail
	// can hold padding — `align8(len) - 8 < len` for every `len > 0` — so one store is the whole of it.
	let tailEnd = emitBin(module, block: zeroPad, a: textBase, b: tail, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let alignBytes = emitConst(module, block: zeroPad, value: DsEntryAlign, ids: ids)
	let lastWord = emitBin(module, block: zeroPad, a: tailEnd, b: alignBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: zeroPad, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(zeroPad, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(lastWord, offset: 0, valueId: zero, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(zeroPad, op: StdOp.branch(copy.id))

	// The tree's ONE byte blit, borrowed rather than written a second time — `__str_copy` is what every
	// String materialization and every `emitBuildOwnedInlineString` already uses, and a `len` of 0 is its
	// own loop guard's answer.
	let copied = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(copy, op: StdOp.call(copied, callee: StrCopyName, args: threeArgs(textBase, second: buffer, third: length)))
	emitCommitAndFinish(module, block: copy, entryPtr: entryPtr, done: done, ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildDsEmitLogText'

// ============================================================================
// The CALL-SITE emitter — the one door the MM runtime reaches this producer through.
// ============================================================================
//
// ⭐ **WHERE THE RUN-TIME HALF OF THE GATING RULE LIVES: the inline `__ds_base == 0` bail, BEFORE any
// call.**
// One load and one branch is the whole cost of an unattached `--debugstream` binary at an event site.
// Because the bail is here, `__ds_reserve` needs no detached path of its own.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT HAS NO COMPILE-TIME GATE OF ITS OWN, AND MUST NOT.** Its four callers — `MmRuntime`'s event
// emitters — each build their arguments out of OPS (a refcount load, a packed-id load, a plus-one), and
// those ops would already be in the block by the time a gate here could decline them. An emitted
// `globalAddr __ds_base` in an untraced build names a `.data` slot that build never laid out, and the
// backend panics at the relocation rather than quietly emitting dead code. So the gate lives at each
// caller, where the arguments are built, and reaching here means the build is traced.
//
// It SPLITS `block` — the caller continues in the block this returns, exactly as `emitPackedElementZero`
// (`ManagedMemoryRuntime`) does.
export function emitMmEvent(module StdModule, func IrFunction, block IrBlock, eventType ParsedInt, packedId ValueId, value ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var emit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"dsEmit")
	let resume = module.addBlock(func, label: b"dsResume")

	let base = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: DsBaseLabel, ids: ids)
	let detached = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let attached = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: base, b: detached, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.condBranch(attached, thenBlockId: emit.id, elseBlockId: resume.id))

	let typeConst = emitConst(module, block: emit, value: eventType, ids: ids)
	let emitted = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(emit, op: StdOp.call(emitted, callee: DsEmitMmName, args: threeArgs(typeConst, second: packedId, third: value)))
	module.setTerminator(emit, op: StdOp.branch(resume.id))

	return resume
end 'emitMmEvent'
